<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sedetani</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 195 BCE (approx.) · capital Salduie</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Iberian people of the middle Ebro around Salduie, the native town on whose site Augustus founded Caesaraugusta, modern Zaragoza.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Sedetani occupied the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> from at least the 5th century BCE. They belonged to the Iberian world, with fortified towns on hills and terraces above the river and its tributaries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their main centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salduie">Salduie</a>, which minted coins with the Iberian legend Salduie. Farming of the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> plain, livestock and trade along the river underpinned their economy. They are sometimes confused in ancient texts with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/edetani">Edetani</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. Horsemen from Salduie served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> and received citizenship in 89 BCE, recorded on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-of-ascoli">Bronze of Ascoli</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Sedetani were brought under Roman control during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato</a>'s campaign of 195 BCE and became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>. Around 14 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-roman-emperor">Augustus</a> founded the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Caesaraugusta</a> on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salduie">Salduie</a>, and the Sedetani merged into Roman provincial society.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sedetani.jpg" alt="Sedetani" width="320" height="312"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:As_de_Salduie_-_reverso.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 195 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salduie</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iberian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Iberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sedetanians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=sedetani" aria-label="Sedetani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4658 694 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sedetani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4978 950L5031 967"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5031 967h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4978 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4978 950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Salduie. <a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=sedetani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
